ivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157481 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157481 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157481 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157481 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "980e0bea-1189-578f-9781-512b76215027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd7463fc-6818-5da6-ab8a-b593c73ec670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157541 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-2x{y}^{3} & x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x{y}^{3})=-2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x{y}^{3})=1-6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{y}^{3} & 1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 1-6x{y}^{2} & -6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,0).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158351 #edb/ref 1061 45105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bac39a49-196c-50bd-99ec-d3c5b74be977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158352 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158352 #edb/ref 1061 45106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bdf28805-f615-5f91-bade-c3cb7038cfd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158353 #edb/ref 1061 45109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e732cfd5-6c10-55ef-b698-a932a1d233c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158354 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158354 #edb/ref 1061 45116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158354 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8222059e-dad9-55b4-8e1d-ac327c86df18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158354 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158568 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to calculate the total derivative of a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R.$ In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate its second derivative.\n\nFor a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n\n![](images/44/44a95860d7e7bd926f4d94c2ebb5b037e8537ac622f7c86b500aa06b9f959250.svg)\n\nthe total derivative of $f$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9f72b67a531f992342e9f9e0b74f447de5e2b62c9fd81f08804096a912e67.svg)\n\nTo differentiate $f$ once more, we first need to take the transpose of the first derivative:\n\n![](images/4d/4d23282171c39149eeebd7301550ebdf1727b86736a57de6e5d7b6ea96891157.svg)\n\nTo calculate the **second derivative** of $f,$ we simply apply the definition of the total derivative to $\\left(\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)\\right)^T.$ By doing this, we get the following $n\\times n$ matrix:\n\n![](images/3d/3d62db0c4018fcce47077285aa05e6c4ea3c79e972ab87fccf5165a07083eda9.svg)\n\nNotice that this matrix is *symmetric* due to the equality of mixed partial derivatives. This becomes important later. \n\nThe matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x)$ is sometimes called the **Hessian matrix,** and its determinant, \n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det\\big( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\big)\n$$\n\nis called the **Hessian determinant**.\n\nNote that in the case of a two-variable function $f(x,y),$ the second derivative is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e83195902ce2cdc2e0ff6c1f51ebc679a4bddd51fedd38c4bc6fddc4c73373dd.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we'll primarily focus on two-variable functions $f(x,y).$ However, we should remember that the techniques discussed here can be applied to scalar functions with an arbitrary number of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158568 #edb/ref 1065 7795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158568 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "79cc27de-f461-55d9-ab0f-3dd5a21dd4f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158568 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236443 #edb/ref 1080 "-{\\pi }^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236443 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236443 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "62d58fc9-233d-5a98-9f47-8a3790a441f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236615 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236615 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236615 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "804c671d-3217-5280-aa02-fe2a73812ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236672 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "88c43743-4bdd-5d6d-8482-949f26f0705c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236758 #edb/ref 1080 "1-2{\\pi }^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "944e0f31-80d6-5eac-8960-7ada905bec43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237192 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\pi }^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d93bce69-5901-5324-bd97-83116ddf04a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186236672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237601 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "77c1b5e6-8017-5a27-ad73-0d376c902134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba2b3d0e-8f3a-5211-b064-2ffdb4af104d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237927 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,-1)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348671 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348671 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348671 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a863fee-48cb-518a-94c7-332f0d5dfb90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348749 #edb/ref 1080 "-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348749 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348749 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "236352e1-e284-5137-93e8-01d0c0b82dd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350254 #edb/ref 1080 "14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350254 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350254 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd6da9cd-0b80-5391-a066-ff87ff441cbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350322 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d5b5d5ad-403a-5e9b-b47e-9495ed54eecd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350365 #edb/ref 1080 "-12" #edb/ref 1759218604